ure of cited evidence to establish a statement is taken as evidence that statement is false.\n- This option suggests that the failure of the cited evidence to establish a statement is taken as evidence that the statement is false. However, the context does not provide any evidence that the statement is false, so this option is not necessarily an error of reasoning.\n\nC) Something that is merely a matter of opinion is treated as if it were subject to verification as a matter of fact.\n- This option suggests that something that is merely a matter of opinion is treated as if it were subject to verification as a matter of fact. However, the context does not provide any evidence that the statement is a matter of opinion, so this option is not necessarily an error of reasoning.\n\nD) Dobson's conclusion logically contradicts some of the evidence presented in support of it.\n- This option suggests that Dobson's conclusion logically contradicts some of the evidence presented in support of it. The context states that the ground for the claim is that two of the stones determine a line pointing directly to the position of the sun at sunrise at the spring equinox. This evidence supports the claim, so Dobson's conclusion does not logically contradict this evidence. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
