messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nA, B, C and D predict the items in the four drawers. A: there are no books in some drawers. B: there are books in all drawers. C: there is no pen in the second drawer. D: there are letters in the third drawer.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, if only one of the four people's conclusions is true, which of the following must be true?\n# Options:\nA) There is a pen in the second drawer.\nB) There are letters in the third drawer.\nC) There are books in all four drawers.\nD) There are no books in the four drawers.. I think the correct option is: B\n", '<think>.\nTo solve this problem, we need to analyze the given statements and determine which one is true while ensuring that only one of them is true. Let\'s break down the information provided:.\n\n1. **Statement A**: "There are no books in some drawers.".\n2. **Statement B**: "There are books in all drawers.".\n3. **Statement C**: "There is no pen in the second drawer.".\n4. **Statement D**: "There are letters in the third drawer.".\n\nWe know that only one of these statements is true. We will evaluate each statement to see if it can be true without contradicting the others.\n\n### Step-by-Step Analysis:.\n\n1. **Assume Statement A is true**:.\n   - If A is true, then there are no books in some drawers. This means that at least one drawer does not contain a book.\n   - If B is true, then there are books in all drawers. This contradicts A because if there are books in all drawers, then there cannot be any drawers without books.\n   - If C is true, then there is no pen in the second drawer. This does not necessarily contradict A or B.\n   - If D is true, then there are letters in the third drawer. This does not necessarily contradict A or B.\n\n   Since assuming A is true leads to a contradiction with B, A cannot be true.\n\n2.